<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Varangian Guard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 988–14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Varangian Guard was the elite bodyguard of the Byzantine emperors, formed around 988 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> received about 6,000 warriors from Vladimir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. At first made up of Rus' and Scandinavians, among them the future king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a>, it later recruited many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> after 1066. Known for their axes and loyalty, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> served until the fourteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/varangian-guard.jpg" alt="Varangian Guard" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_body_of_Leo_V_is_dragged_to_the_Hippodrome_through_the_Skyla_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> were the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> in Byzantine service. A treaty of 874 after a period of hostilities obliged the Rus' to provide men, and Varangians are recorded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in 911. In 988 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir I of Kiev</a> for help to defend his throne, and under an earlier treaty Vladimir sent 6,000 men, taking the chance to rid himself of unruly warriors he could not pay. In exchange he received Basil's sister Anna in marriage and agreed to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Basil distrusted the native guardsmen, whose loyalties often shifted, and kept the Varangians as his personal guard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 989 these men landed at Chrysopolis with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> and defeated the rebel Bardas Phokas, who died of a stroke on the field. The guard served extensively in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the 11th century, winning the Battle of Cannae in 1018, and in the reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-maniakes">George Maniakes</a> in 1038, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a> was a prominent member. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> in 1071 virtually all the emperor's guards fell around him. Until the late 11th century the guard was almost entirely Norse, with so many Swedish men enlisting that a medieval Swedish law forbade anyone to inherit while staying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman conquest of England</a> many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> joined, and by the time of Alexios Komnenos the guard was largely recruited from them and others who had suffered at the hands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> and Normans. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos">John II Komnenos</a> win at Beroia in 1122, and they defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the Fourth Crusade. By the late 13th century most were assimilated, though the guard lasted to at least the mid-14th century and up to 3,000 are said to have stood in the city in 1453.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> swore an oath of loyalty to the emperor, performed ceremonial duties as retainers and acclaimers, and carried out some police duties in cases of treason and conspiracy. They were headed by an officer called the akolouthos, usually a native Byzantine. Their main weapon was the broad-bladed Dane axe, and Byzantine writers called them axe-bearing barbarians. They were used in battle at critical moments.</p><p>Their loyalty became a trope of Byzantine writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-komnene">Anna Komnene</a>, but it attached to the office and not the man: in 969 they knelt to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John Tzimiskes</a> after he killed Nikephoros II. Snorri Sturluson says that on an emperor's death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> could take as much gold as they could carry, but Byzantine sources do not mention it and modern historians doubt it. Their service is also recorded on Scandinavian runestones, such as the Greece Runestones raised by former members or in their memory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Varangian Guard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vladimir the Great built lines of earthworks and forts south of Kiev against the Pechenegs and sent about 6,000 warriors to help Basil II in 988, the origin of the Byzantine <b>Varangian Guard</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Harald Hardrada (r. 1046–1066), a veteran of the Byzantine <b>Varangian Guard</b>, fought a long war against Denmark before invading England, where he was killed at the Battle of Stamford Bridge in 1066.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From about 988 Scandinavians served the Byzantine emperors in the <b>Varangian Guard</b>, and a marble lion from Piraeus, taken to Venice in 1687, still bears runes probably cut by such men.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Runic graffiti in the northern gallery are believed to have been left by members of the <b>Varangian Guard</b>, and a 9th-century Viking inscription in the southern section reads “Halvdan was here”.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Beside the provincial soldiers stood the professional regiments (tagmata) of Constantinople and, from about 988, the <b>Varangian Guard</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>So many Swedes joined the Byzantine emperor’s <b>Varangian Guard</b>, formed in the late 10th century, that a law of Västergötland barred anyone staying in “Greece” from inheriting, and Cnut’s conquest brought a royal guard of housecarls to England, which fought for Harold Godwinson at the Battle of Hastings…</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li></ul></section>
